qs.append({"q":"Between 1885 and 1892, the nationalist demand for constitutional reforms centred on which two objectives?","o":["Expansion of councils (greater Indian participation) and reform of councils (more powers, especially over finances)","Complete abolition of the councils and direct rule by Indian princes","Introduction of universal adult franchise and a bicameral legislature","Formation of a constituent assembly and a written constitution"],"a":0,"e":"The nationalists' demands during this period focused on expanding council membership for Indians and reforming councils to grant them greater powers, especially budgetary control."})

qs.append({"q":"The nationalist slogan \"No taxation without representation\" reflected their demand for:","o":["Control over the budget, including the power to vote upon and amend it","Abolition of the salt tax alone","Reservation of seats for women in legislatures","Reduction of import duties on British goods"],"a":0,"e":"The slogan captured the demand that Indians should have real budgetary control in the legislature, not merely nominal representation without financial power."})

qs.append({"q":"Which of the following leaders demanded self-government on the lines of self-governing colonies like Canada and Australia, in the years indicated?\n1. Dadabhai Naoroji (1904)\n2. Gopal Krishna Gokhale (1905)\n3. Lokmanya Tilak (1906)\nHow many of the above are correctly matched?","o":["All three","Only two","Only one","None"],"a":0,"e":"All three leaders — Naoroji (1904), Gokhale (1905) and Tilak (1906) — demanded colonial self-government on the Canadian/Australian model."})

