qs.append({"q":"The 1897 arrest of Bal Gangadhar Tilak and other leaders/journalists, along with the deportation of the Natu brothers without trial, is cited in the chapter as an example of:","o":["Increased public consciousness around the defence of civil rights becoming integral to the freedom struggle","The complete suppression of nationalist activity by 1900","The beginning of the Non-Cooperation Movement","The formal split between Moderates and Extremists"],"a":0,"e":"These incidents galvanised public outrage and consciousness about civil rights (speech, association, free press) as a core concern of the nationalist movement."})

qs.append({"q":"Which of the following is a criticism levelled against the early nationalists (Moderates), as acknowledged in the chapter's evaluation?","o":["They failed to widen their democratic base by not including the masses, especially women, and not demanding universal suffrage","They had no interest whatsoever in economic issues","They supported armed revolution against the British","They refused to engage with the legislative councils at all"],"a":0,"e":"Despite their achievements, the Moderates are criticised for a narrow social base — they did not mobilise the masses (including women) or demand universal suffrage."})

qs.append({"q":"According to the chapter's evaluation of the early nationalists, which of the following is NOT listed as one of their positive contributions?","o":["They organised large-scale peasant uprisings against zamindars","They created a wide national awakening based on common interests against a common enemy","They exposed the exploitative character of colonial rule","They trained people in political work and popularised modern ideas"],"a":0,"e":"The early nationalists did not organise mass peasant uprisings; their work was largely constitutional, elite-driven, and focused on political education, exposing colonial exploitation, and fostering national awakening."})

